    <body>
      <sec id="article-26105.s1" sec-type="Definition/Introduction">
        <title>Definition/Introduction</title>
        <p content-type="pubmed-excerpt">Occupational therapy is sometimes confused with physical therapy; however, it is fundamentally an assessment and intervention approach that supports individuals through the therapeutic use of everyday activities. The process typically begins with an assessment, followed by a tailored intervention plan designed to help individuals maintain, recover, or develop meaningful activities or occupations. See&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Occupational Therapy.</p>
        <p>This service is frequently provided in long-term care settings and is delivered by occupational therapists and occupational therapy assistants. An often overlooked component of occupational therapy is its focus on mental health and well-being, particularly important because many residents in long-term care facilities have a diagnosis of depression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Interventions, whether individual or group, address the whole person to enhance physical, psychological, and social adaptation, ultimately supporting quality of life even in the presence of disability.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26105.s2" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>Occupations are the set of activities that are significant to the patient's cultural context, age-appropriate, chosen, organized, and carried out by each individual to provide for himself, to derive joy in living, and to contribute to the economic and social life of the community (Canadian Association of Occupational Therapy, 1997). Occupation is the goal of occupational therapy and the means by which we seek to modify a person's body functions (motor-sensory, perceptual-cognitive, and emotional-relational abilities). Generally, there are&#x000a0;3 areas in which we can find occupations: personal care, work (school), and free time. Occupational therapy is practiced in a wide range of settings, including hospitals, health centers, the home, workplaces, schools, and retirement homes&#x000a0;(World Federation of Occupational Therapy).&#x000a0;Occupational therapists adopt a person-centered approach and practice based on evidence (evidence-based health care).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Mobility: Walking and Moving Around</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Activities of Daily Living (ADL) Index:&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is usually done through both interviews and observation. The observation component includes tasks such as toileting, personal grooming and hygiene, eating and drinking, dressing, and moving independently throughout the house. This is scored based on whether the patient can perform these tasks independently or requires assistance from another person.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">AM-PAC:&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>AM-PAC stands for "Activity Measure for Post-Acute Care."&#x000a0;The AM-PAC is an outcomes instrument that measures&#x000a0;3 domains: applied cognitive, basic mobility, and daily activities. This is often used to monitor and research outpatient rehabilitation, nursing home, and long-term acute care (LTAC) settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r2">[2]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Modified Berthel Index:&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>MBI is used to assess activities of daily living across 10 domains. These include bathing, feeding, stair climbing, personal hygiene, dressing, bowel control, bladder control, chair transfer, and ambulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r3">[3]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Stroke Impact Scale:&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This self-reported questionnaire can evaluate patients' disability and quality of life after stroke.<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>It takes about 15&#x000a0;to 20 minutes to administer and requires no training. It covers&#x000a0;8 domains: memory and cognition, emotion, strength, communication, ADLs, mobility, hand function, and participation. Each item is scored from 1&#x000a0;to 5. A lower score signifies more ease in completing the task.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Self Care</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Cleveland Scale of Activities of Daily Living</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is a 2-factor model that assesses basic activities of daily living in individuals with dementia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r5">[5]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional Assessment Scale&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is a checklist-type scale that is used for institutionalized patients.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Klein-Bell Activities of Daily Living Scale</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This scale has been developed to assess independent functioning for research and clinical purposes. The interrater reliability of this test is 92%, regardless of training level.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r6">[6]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Melville-Nelson Self-Care Assessment</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is a scale used by occupational therapists in subacute rehabilitation or skilled nursing facilities (SNFs). The interrater reliability was 94%. The&#x000a0;7 domains are bed mobility, transfers, dressing, eating, toileting, personal hygiene, and bathing. Each of these is divided into suboccupations. For instance, personal hygiene may be divided into hair brushing, handwashing, dental care, and even self-care. Those are then divided into sub-suboccupations. Dental hygiene may be subdivided into the right, left, upper, and lower arches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r7">[7]</xref>occupations (bed mobility, transfers, dressing, eating, toileting, personal hygiene, bathing) are rated in&#x000a0;2 ways: self-performance (how much the person did) and support (the&#x000a0;degree of assistance needed from others)&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Modified Barthel ADL Index</italic>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">AM-PAC</italic>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Swallowing</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">The Mann Assessment of Swallowing Ability (MASA)</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This was first introduced to identify dysphagia in acute-stage stroke patients. It can now be used as a screening tool for any dependent older adult.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Acute Stroke Dysphagia Screen</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is an easily administered and reliable tool that can be used to detect both dysphagia and aspiration risk in acute stroke patients. The interrater reliability is 93.6%, and the test-retest reliability was 92.5%. It also has a sensitivity&#x0003e;90% and a specificity&#x0003e;68% for detecting dysphagia and aspiration.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Victorian Dysphagia Screening Model ASSIST Tool</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Completion of this is recommended by personnel who have completed approved dysphagia screening training. This is recommended in the presence of persistent acute stroke symptoms.&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Swallowing Ability and Function Evaluation</italic>
<bold>
</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This test evaluates swallowing. Before this test is administered, a baseline evaluation of cognition and proper motor function should be assessed.&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Attention&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Test of Everyday Attention (TEA)</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The TEA is an attention test that provides an overview by breaking attention into distinct factors. This is achieved by including subtests, such as searching a telephone directory or listening to lottery results, that mimic everyday tasks. This is a useful assessment tool for work rehabilitation programs, particularly for jobs that require sustained attention, such as air traffic control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r9">[9]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Neurobehavioral Cognitive Status Screening Examination (COGNISTAT)</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This assessment provides a brief screening of cognitive dysfunction. It provides profile scores for language constructions, memory, calculations, and reasoning. It also separately and independently assesses orientation, level of consciousness, and attention.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">D2 Test of Attention&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This assessment is a cancellation test that can measure attention, visual scanning, and processing speed. It is a paper-and-pencil test that assesses whether the patient can focus attention on specific numbers, letters, and combinations of the&#x000a0;2 over a specified period.&#x000a0;Cancellation test to measure attention, visual scanning, and processing speed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r10">[10]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Memory</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Contextual Memory Test&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This assessment tool can measure both memory and metamemory in individuals with cognitive disabilities. The domains assessed include memory capacity, strategy of use, and recall.&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Rivermead Behavioral Memory Test</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This assessment identifies everyday problems and is good at tracking change over time. The RBMT can help distinguish between MCI and mild dementia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r11">[11]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">AM-PAC</italic>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Carrying Objects</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional Reach Test</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A valuable tool for individuals at risk of falls. It can test AP (anteroposterior) stability.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Action Research Arm Test&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This test can assess limitations in upper-limb mobility following a brain insult. It should be used in people with a TBI (traumatic brain injury).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;The scores from 19 specific tests are summed. These 19 specifics are spread along 4 specific subsets (grasp, grip, pincer, and gross movement). The 19 tests are given a number 0-3. 0 = no movement, 1 = the movement is partially performed, 2 = the movement is completed but takes a long time, and 3 = the movement is performed normally.&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">The Arm Motor Ability Test (AMAT)&#x000a0;</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is also a test to identify and assess upper extremity limitations quantitatively in patients who have had a stroke or any other insult to their brain.&#x000a0;A study showed that a change of 0.44 or greater in the AMAT shows a clinically meaningful and statistically significant change. AMAT assesses functional ability, quality of movement, and performance time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r13">[13]</xref><italic toggle="yes"/></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Changing and Maintaining Body Position</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Assessment of Motor and Process Skills (AMPS)</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This assessment takes time to administer. Those evaluated with AMPS are rated on 15 motor skills and 20 process skill items after being observed performing&#x000a0;2 or&#x000a0;3 IADLs. This provides an objective assessment of both motor and process skills.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Berg Balance Scale</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This assessment, developed in 1989, was designed to identify and measure balance in older adults. It measures both static and dynamic abilities through functional tasks commonly performed in everyday life. The scale has 14 items, and each is scored on a scale of 0&#x000a0;to 4 with a total of 52 points. The higher the score, the better the balance. The interrater reliability is 97%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r15">[15]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<italic toggle="yes">Timed Get Up and Go Test</italic>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The patient is observed and assessed as they rise from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit. The&#x000a0;patient is timed during this test, and it has good interrater reliability. This aligns well with the previous Berg Balance Scale and can predict whether it would be safe for the patient to go outside.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r16">[16]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26105.s3" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The goal of rehabilitation, especially occupational therapy, is to help those return to the activities that they need and want to do. To this end, it is important to apply current research, evidence, and critical reasoning to achieve specific outcomes better. Formalized assessments are important because they&#x000a0;reduce bias, which is essential for accurately classifying specific categories under assessment. These specific assessments listed above are important because they serve as a way to identify any deviations from 'normal.' These deviations can then be analyzed, and specific therapies can be used to address the patient/client's needs. Finally, the assessments provide a means to measure change over time and to track the progress the patient/client has made.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Occupational therapists work with individuals of all ages, from children to the elderly. The first appointment with an occupational therapist typically involves an assessment of the situation. This includes the history of any condition the patient has had and, in particular, an evaluation of the difference between what could be done in the past and what can be done now. Goal setting is an important part of occupational therapy. The occupational therapist works with the patient to determine which activities are most important to him and to establish clear goals to improve his ability to perform them. What often distinguishes occupational therapy from physiotherapy is that, while a physical therapist focuses on improving physical function and movement, an occupational therapist seeks to determine whether there is an alternative way to perform a given activity. Occupational therapy is, therefore, more oriented toward adaptation.</p>
        <p>Another important distinction between physiotherapists and occupational therapists is that the former work with patients in both acute and chronic phases, whereas the latter work with patients with chronic or resolving conditions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26105.s4" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>The occupational therapist works within a multidisciplinary team, which is the most effective strategy for the patient's well-being.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26105.s5" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring</title>
        <p>In multidisciplinary treatment, each figure who follows the patient's care and well-being must monitor outcomes using specific, possibly validated scales; this approach provides a more rigorous measure of the results obtained during the patient's recovery trajectory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26105.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26105.s6">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26105&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26105">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/articlelibrary/commentarticle/26105/?utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=comments&#x00026;utm_content=26105">Comment on this article.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
    </body>
